.TH KASLD 1 "2026-07-25" "kasld 0.3.1\-dev" "KASLD Manual"
.SH NAME
kasld \- recover the Linux kernel memory layout and defeat KASLR
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kasld
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kasld
recovers the Linux kernel's virtual and physical memory layout \(em primarily
the kernel text base \(em from a local process, using as much as the process's
vantage allows: its privileges and capabilities, the system's configuration,
and any container confinement.
It recovers the kernel text base outright where a leak or side channel allows,
and otherwise narrows it to the smallest set of placements the available
evidence supports.
.PP
An inference engine fuses evidence from dozens of independent techniques with
the architecture's known invariants, narrowing the kernel's placement to a
residual window \(em reported as the surviving slot count and bits of entropy.
That residual window is an upper bound on the protection KASLR retains from
this vantage, not a guarantee the base is beyond an attacker's reach; see
.I limitations.md
under the installed documentation.
On architectures without KASLR the engine locates the bootloader-chosen load
address.
.PP
Every quantity in the readout carries a grade.
.B guaranteed
names the window the evidence proves contains the true value: it is resolved
only from facts at or above the soundness floor, so the truth is never outside
it.
.B likely
names a narrower answer drawn from every available signal, including timing
side channels and configuration heuristics \(em usually correct, and always
contained within the guaranteed window, but capable of being wrong.
A concrete base is reported as
.B likely
whenever the guaranteed window is still a range, so the grade, not the width,
is what separates a proven address from a good guess.
.PP
Each leak technique is a standalone component binary, discovered and executed
at run time (see
.B FILES
and
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
The default output is a compact text readout; alternative machine-readable
formats are selected with the options below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output format (mutually exclusive)
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-oneline
Single-line summary output.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-markdown
Markdown table output.
.SS Output detail
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show component output, the KASLR analysis, and the memory map.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-explain
Show technique explanations before each component (implies
.BR \-\-verbose ).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the banner, progress, and warnings.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-color
Colourize text output. Auto-detected for TTYs and suppressed by the
.B NO_COLOR
and
.B CLICOLOR
environment variables (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-ascii
ASCII-only output: replace the Unicode status glyphs and the box-art banner
with ASCII equivalents. Auto-enabled in a non-UTF-8 locale; this flag forces it
regardless (for screen readers on a UTF-8 system). The JSON
.RB ( \-j )
and one-line
.RB ( \-1 )
formats carry no glyphs and are unaffected \(em they are the fully accessible
interfaces.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-hardening
Append the hardening assessment to text and markdown output.
.TP
.B \-\-map
Draw the address-space diagram \(em the virtual and physical region layout with
gaps and leak positions \(em without the per-component output that
.B \-\-verbose
also enables.
.B \-\-verbose
implies it. Carried by text and markdown output.
.SS Component control
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-experimental
Enable experimental components.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " "\-\-skip " \fIPATTERN\fR
Skip matching components (glob, comma-separated; the flag may repeat).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " "\-\-timeout " \fIN\fR
Per-component timeout in seconds (default: 30).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fast
Shortcut for a 2-second per-component timeout
.RB ( \-\-timeout
wins if both are given).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " "\-\-workers " \fIN\fR
Parallel component workers (default: the number of online CPUs; 0 = sequential).
.SS Miscellaneous
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.PP
Short flags may be bundled:
.B \-fq
is
.BR "\-f \-q" .
A value-taking flag
.RB ( \-s ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-w )
may appear only last in a bundle, taking the next argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Ran to completion and recovered at least one leak result.
.TP
.B 1
Ran to completion but no leak produced a result. This is a valid outcome, not
an error: a hardened host that yields nothing is the success case. The KASLR
window analysis and, with
.BR \-H ,
the hardening assessment are still reported.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, conflicting output-format flags, or the component directory could
not be found.
.TP
.B 3
The target kernel addresses memory more widely than this build can represent:
a 32-bit build against a 64-bit kernel, which is the normal arrangement on a
device with a 64-bit kernel and a 32-bit userland. No analysis is attempted,
because the layout model comes from the build's own architecture and every
window it resolved would describe an address space the kernel does not have.
Run the build matching the kernel's word size.
.IP
The reason is written to standard error in every mode. What reaches standard
output differs by format: the text modes write nothing there;
.BR \-m " and " \-j
write a short document naming the refusal, carrying no layout or KASLR fields
so that nothing in it can be read as a measurement; and
.B \-1
writes nothing, because that format fixes its key set on every line and a line
of
.B na
values would be indistinguishable from the hardened host that yields no results
(exit 1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Two of these name programs that
.B kasld
will execute:
.B KASLD_COMPONENT_DIR
selects the directory every component binary is run from, and
.B KASLD_EXEC_WRAPPER
selects a program run in their place. Preserving
.B KASLD_*
across a privilege boundary therefore hands over code execution at that
privilege.
.B sudo
strips them under its default
.BR env_reset ;
a
.B sudoers
rule with
.B env_keep
or
.BR SETENV ,
or any wrapper that elevates while keeping the environment, does not.
.B kasld
covers the remaining case itself: where the kernel marks the exec as
privilege-gaining \(em a set-uid or set-gid binary, or one carrying file
capabilities \(em every
.B KASLD_*
variable is dropped before it is read, and the run proceeds on its own
configuration, reporting that it did so on standard error.
.TP
.B KASLD_COMPONENT_DIR
Directory to load component binaries from, overriding the default search
(see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B KASLD_SYSROOT
Treat this path as the filesystem root when reading kernel facts, for offline
replay of a captured tree. Components that depend on live runtime state are
skipped.
.TP
.B KASLD_EXPERIMENTAL
When set, enable experimental components, equivalent to
.BR \-\-experimental .
.TP
.B KASLD_EXEC_WRAPPER
Program to execute in place of each component, with the component's path as
its first argument. Intended for running guest-architecture components under
a user-mode emulator. Unset or empty gives the normal path, a direct
.BR execve (2)
of the component.
.TP
.B KASLD_VERBOSE
When set to a value that is non-empty and does not begin with
.BR 0 ,
enable verbose output, equivalent to
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.B KASLD_UNAME_RELEASE
Override the kernel release reported by
.BR uname (2),
so a captured tree replays under the release it was taken from rather than
the running one. Used with
.BR KASLD_SYSROOT .
.TP
.B KASLD_DEBUG_CONSTRAINTS
When set, print each inference constraint and the resulting bounds to standard
error. Diagnostic only; it does not affect the result.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When present (any value, including empty), disable coloured output regardless of
whether stdout is a TTY. An explicit
.B \-\-color
or
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
overrides it. See
.IR https://no-color.org .
.TP
.BR CLICOLOR ", " CLICOLOR_FORCE
.B CLICOLOR=0
disables colour;
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
(non-empty and not
.BR 0 )
forces colour on even when stdout is not a TTY (BSD convention).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <bindir>/../libexec/kasld/
Installed component binaries (FHS layout). The search order is
.B KASLD_COMPONENT_DIR
first, then
.I components/
beside the
.B kasld
binary, then
.I ../libexec/kasld/
relative to it.
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/doc/kasld/
Installed documentation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Default text readout:
.PP
.RS
.EX
kasld
.EE
.RE
.PP
Machine-readable JSON, with the hardening assessment:
.PP
.RS
.EX
kasld \-H \-j
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pipe the one-line summary into
.BR ksymoff (1)
to resolve a symbol at its runtime address:
.PP
.RS
.EX
kasld \-1 | ksymoff \-s System.map commit_creds
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ksymoff (1)
.PP
The guides under
.I <prefix>/share/doc/kasld/
cover usage, exploitation, and the inference engine in depth.
